(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("gpu-serving",{"gpu-serving/gpu-basics-memory-and-what-limits-inference":{"html":"\u003cp>A large language model is mostly a very big list of numbers that must be read for every word it produces. Whether it runs, how fast, and for how many users depends on a few simple sums. This lesson gives you the sums.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What a GPU is good at and what its memory looks like\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to estimate memory for weights and the KV cache\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Prefill versus decode, and why decode is bound by memory bandwidth\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Quantization and what it trades\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-a-gpu-offers\">What a GPU offers\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A GPU has thousands of simple cores that do many multiplications at once, which suits the matrix arithmetic in neural networks. It has its own fast memory called \u003cstrong>VRAM\u003c/strong> (or HBM on data center cards). A model must fit in VRAM to run well. Data centre cards range from 24 GB to 80 GB or more per device, and a model that does not fit must be split across several cards or compressed.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"weights\">Weights\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Memory for the model&#39;s \u003cstrong>weights\u003c/strong> is the parameter count times the bytes per parameter.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Precision\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Bytes per parameter\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>8B model\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>70B model\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>FP16 or BF16\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>2\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>about 16 GB\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>about 140 GB\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>INT8 / FP8\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>1\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>about 8 GB\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>about 70 GB\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>4-bit\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>about 0.5\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>about 4 to 5 GB\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>about 35 to 40 GB\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A 70B model in 16-bit needs more than one 80 GB card. In 4-bit it fits on one, with some loss of quality that depends on the method and model.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-kv-cache\">The KV cache\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>While generating, the model keeps the \u003cstrong>key and value\u003c/strong> vectors of every token seen so far, so it does not recompute them. This is the \u003cstrong>KV cache\u003c/strong>, and it grows with each token and each concurrent request.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Bytes per token = 2 (key and value) × layers × KV heads × head size × bytes per value.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>For a model with 32 layers, 8 KV heads (grouped-query attention) and head size 128 in 16-bit:\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>2 × 32 × 8 × 128 × 2 = 131 072 bytes, which is 128 KiB per token.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A 4 000-token conversation takes about 0.5 GB, and 64 such conversations take about 32 GB. That is why serving many users needs far more memory than the weights alone, and why memory runs out before compute does.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"prefill-and-decode\">Prefill and decode\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Generating a reply has two phases.\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Prefill\u003c/strong> reads the whole prompt at once. It is compute-heavy and fast per token, and it sets the \u003cstrong>time to first token (TTFT)\u003c/strong>.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Decode\u003c/strong> produces one token at a time. For each token, the GPU must read all the weights from memory. The speed is therefore close to memory bandwidth divided by model size.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>A rough ceiling for one stream: a card with 2 000 GB/s of bandwidth and a 16 GB model can read the weights about 125 times per second, so about 125 tokens per second for one user, in theory. In practice you get less. \u003cstrong>Batching\u003c/strong> serves many users with the same read of the weights, which is how throughput rises. That is why batch size and memory for the cache decide cost per token.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"quantization\">Quantization\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Quantization\u003c/strong> stores weights, and sometimes the KV cache, with fewer bits. It reduces memory and increases speed, because less data is read per token. Check quality on your own tasks before using an aggressive setting: small models and tasks needing exact numbers are more sensitive.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"nvidia-smi --query-gpu=name,memory.total,memory.used --format=csv\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>nvidia-smi --query-gpu=name,memory.total,memory.used --format=csv\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">name, memory.total [MiB], memory.used [MiB]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NVIDIA A100-SXM4-80GB, 81920 MiB, 40213 MiB\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-model-that-loaded-and-then-crashed\">Incident: The model that loaded and then crashed\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A 13B model loaded fine on a 24 GB card and crashed under the first real traffic.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"nvidia-smi --query-gpu=memory.used,memory.total --format=csv,noheader\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>nvidia-smi --query-gpu=memory.used,memory.total --format=csv,noheader\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">23410 MiB, 24576 MiB\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">torch.OutOfMemoryError: CUDA out of memory. Tried to allocate 1.20 GiB.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">GPU 0 has a total capacity of 24.00 GiB of which 0.81 GiB is free.\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The weights in 16-bit took about 26 GB, so the team had loaded an 8-bit version of about 13 GB, which left about 10 GB. That looked like plenty in testing with one request. Under real traffic the KV cache of many long prompts used all of it. They reduced the maximum context length, limited concurrent requests, and moved to a larger card. Memory has to be planned for weights plus cache at peak load, not at the first test.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Sizing a GPU by weights only\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Assuming more GPU cores will fix slow decoding when bandwidth is the limit\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using aggressive quantization with no quality test\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Testing with one request and shipping\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"what-a-gpu-offers","text":"What a GPU offers"},{"id":"weights","text":"Weights"},{"id":"the-kv-cache","text":"The KV cache"},{"id":"prefill-and-decode","text":"Prefill and decode"},{"id":"quantization","text":"Quantization"},{"id":"incident-the-model-that-loaded-and-then-crashed","text":"Incident: The model that loaded and then crashed"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"NVIDIA: CUDA documentation","u":"https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/"},{"t":"Hugging Face: LLM inference optimization","u":"https://huggingface.co/docs/transformers/llm_tutorial_optimization"},{"t":"vLLM documentation","u":"https://docs.vllm.ai/en/latest/"}],"text":"A large language model is mostly a very big list of numbers that must be read for every word it produces. Whether it runs, how fast, and for how many users depends on a few simple sums. This lesson gives you the sums. What you will learn What a GPU is good at and what its memory looks like How to estimate memory for weights and the KV cache Prefill versus decode, and why decode is bound by memory bandwidth Quantization and what it trades What a GPU offers A GPU has thousands of simple cores that do many multiplications at once, which suits the matrix arithmetic in neural networks. It has its own fast memory called VRAM (or HBM on data center cards). A model must fit in VRAM to run well. Data centre cards range from 24 GB to 80 GB or more per device, and a model that does not fit must be split across several cards or compressed. Weights Memory for the model&#39;s weights is the parameter count times the bytes per parameter. Precision Bytes per parameter 8B model 70B model FP16 or BF16 2 about 16 GB about 140 GB INT8 / FP8 1 about 8 GB about 70 GB 4-bit about 0.5 about 4 to 5 GB about 35 to 40 GB A 70B model in 16-bit needs more than one 80 GB card. In 4-bit it fits on one, with some loss of quality that depends on the method and model. The KV cache While generating, the model keeps the key and value vectors of every token seen so far, so it does not recompute them. This is the KV cache , and it grows with each token and each concurrent request. Bytes per token = 2 (key and value) × layers × KV heads × head size × bytes per value. For a model with 32 layers, 8 KV heads (grouped-query attention) and head size 128 in 16-bit: 2 × 32 × 8 × 128 × 2 = 131 072 bytes, which is 128 KiB per token. A 4 000-token conversation takes about 0.5 GB, and 64 such conversations take about 32 GB. That is why serving many users needs far more memory than the weights alone, and why memory runs out before compute does. Prefill and decode Generating a reply has two phases. Prefill reads the whole prompt at once. It is compute-heavy and fast per token, and it sets the time to first token (TTFT) . Decode produces one token at a time. For each token, the GPU must read all the weights from memory. The speed is therefore close to memory bandwidth divided by model size. A rough ceiling for one stream: a card with 2 000 GB/s of bandwidth and a 16 GB model can read the weights about 125 times per second, so about 125 tokens per second for one user, in theory. In practice you get less. Batching serves many users with the same read of the weights, which is how throughput rises. That is why batch size and memory for the cache decide cost per token. Quantization Quantization stores weights, and sometimes the KV cache, with fewer bits. It reduces memory and increases speed, because less data is read per token. Check quality on your own tasks before using an aggressive setting: small models and tasks needing exact numbers are more sensitive. $ nvidia-smi --query-gpu=name,memory.total,memory.used --format=csv name, memory.total [MiB], memory.used [MiB] NVIDIA A100-SXM4-80GB, 81920 MiB, 40213 MiB Incident: The model that loaded and then crashed Incident A 13B model loaded fine on a 24 GB card and crashed under the first real traffic. $ nvidia-smi --query-gpu=memory.used,memory.total --format=csv,noheader 23410 MiB, 24576 MiB torch.OutOfMemoryError: CUDA out of memory. Tried to allocate 1.20 GiB. GPU 0 has a total capacity of 24.00 GiB of which 0.81 GiB is free. The weights in 16-bit took about 26 GB, so the team had loaded an 8-bit version of about 13 GB, which left about 10 GB. That looked like plenty in testing with one request. Under real traffic the KV cache of many long prompts used all of it. They reduced the maximum context length, limited concurrent requests, and moved to a larger card. Memory has to be planned for weights plus cache at peak load, not at the first test. Common mistakes Sizing a GPU by weights only Assuming more GPU cores will fix slow decoding"},"gpu-serving/serving-stacks-vllm-tgi-triton":{"html":"\u003cp>Running a model in a notebook is easy. Serving it to many people at once, with steady latency and good use of the GPU, needs a serving engine.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Why a plain loop wastes the GPU\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Continuous batching and PagedAttention\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The main engines: vLLM, TGI, TensorRT-LLM and Triton\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The vLLM flags you will tune\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"why-a-loop-is-not-enough\">Why a loop is not enough\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A simple server handles one request, or a fixed batch of requests, at a time. Replies have different lengths, so a fixed batch waits for the longest one while the GPU idles for the finished ones. New requests wait until the batch ends.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Continuous batching\u003c/strong> (also called in-flight batching) fixes this. After every generated token, the engine can drop finished sequences and add waiting ones, so the GPU stays full and new users start sooner.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>PagedAttention\u003c/strong>, used by vLLM, stores the KV cache in small fixed pages instead of one large block per request. This reduces wasted memory from fragmentation, so more requests fit in the same GPU. \u003cstrong>Prefix caching\u003c/strong> reuses the cache for a shared prompt start, such as a long system prompt.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-engines\">The engines\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>vLLM\u003c/strong>: an open-source engine with continuous batching, PagedAttention and an OpenAI-compatible HTTP API. A popular default for open models.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Text Generation Inference (TGI)\u003c/strong>: Hugging Face&#39;s server with similar features, tied to the Hugging Face ecosystem.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>TensorRT-LLM\u003c/strong>: NVIDIA&#39;s library that compiles models into optimised kernels for NVIDIA GPUs. It can be the fastest on those cards, with more setup work.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Triton Inference Server\u003c/strong>: NVIDIA&#39;s general serving platform for many model types, which can host TensorRT-LLM and other backends.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Hosted APIs\u003c/strong>: the provider runs the stack, and you pay per token.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Choose by model support, hardware, operational skill and how much you want to tune. Many teams start with vLLM and measure.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-vllm-example\">A vLLM example\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"vllm serve meta-llama/Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct \\\n    --max-model-len 8192 \\\n    --gpu-memory-utilization 0.90 \\\n    --max-num-seqs 64\ncurl -s localhost:8000/v1/chat/completions -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\\n    -d '{&quot;model&quot;:&quot;meta-llama/Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct&quot;,&quot;messages&quot;:[{&quot;role&quot;:&quot;user&quot;,&quot;content&quot;:&quot;hello&quot;}],&quot;max_tokens&quot;:20}'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>vllm serve meta-llama/Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --max-model-len 8192 \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --gpu-memory-utilization 0.90 \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --max-num-seqs 64\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">INFO  Loading model weights took 14.99 GiB\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">INFO  GPU KV cache size: 41,216 tokens\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">INFO  Maximum concurrency for 8192 tokens per request: 5.03x\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">INFO  Uvicorn running on http://0.0.0.0:8000\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -s localhost:8000/v1/chat/completions -H \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Content-Type: application/json&#x27;\u003c/span> \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    -d \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{&quot;model&quot;:&quot;meta-llama/Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct&quot;,&quot;messages&quot;:[{&quot;role&quot;:&quot;user&quot;,&quot;content&quot;:&quot;hello&quot;}],&quot;max_tokens&quot;:20}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Key settings:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>--max-model-len\u003c/code>: the longest context accepted. Setting it higher makes the engine reserve for longer requests and reduces how many fit.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>--gpu-memory-utilization\u003c/code>: the fraction of VRAM the engine may use, including the cache. If other processes share the GPU, lower it.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>--max-num-seqs\u003c/code>: how many sequences run together.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>--tensor-parallel-size N\u003c/code>: split each layer across N GPUs, needed when the model is larger than one card. It needs fast links between GPUs.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>The log line &quot;GPU KV cache size&quot; is the most useful number at startup. It says how many tokens of cache the memory can hold, and so how many concurrent users with how long a context.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"operating-notes\">Operating notes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Model weights are large (tens of GB). Cache them on a persistent volume so a restart does not download them again.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Start-up takes minutes. Use readiness probes, and do not send traffic until the model is loaded.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Pin the engine version, and test an upgrade, because behaviour and defaults change.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-server-that-was-slow-with-one-gpu-idle\">Incident: The server that was slow with one GPU idle\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A two-GPU server delivered only the speed of one.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"nvidia-smi --query-gpu=index,utilization.gpu,memory.used --format=csv,noheader\nps aux | grep vllm | head -1\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>nvidia-smi --query-gpu=index,utilization.gpu,memory.used --format=csv,noheader\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">0, 98 %, 76210 MiB\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">1, 0 %, 4 MiB\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ps aux | grep vllm | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">vllm serve mistralai/Mixtral-8x7B-Instruct-v0.1 --max-model-len 16384\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The engine had been started without \u003ccode>--tensor-parallel-size 2\u003c/code>, so it used only the first GPU, squeezed the model and the cache into it, and left the second card empty. After the flag was added the model was split over both cards, the cache became larger and throughput roughly doubled. The monitoring that revealed it was the per-GPU utilization, which had not been on the dashboard.\u003c/p>\n\u003csection class=\"wp\" aria-label=\"Worked example with a wrong path\">\u003ch4 class=\"wp-h\">Worked example: a tempting wrong path\u003c/h4>\u003cp class=\"wp-goal\">\u003cstrong>Goal.\u003c/strong> Process each request alone for predictable latency.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-bad\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The tempting way\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode># server runs with a batch size of 1\nvllm serve model --max-num-seqs 1\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See what goes wrong\u003c/summary>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>$ nvidia-smi\nGPU util 18%\nthroughput: 2 requests/s, queue growing\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp class=\"wp-why\">\u003cstrong>Why it fails.\u003c/strong> A GPU is built to do many things at once. With one request at a time most of its capacity is idle and the queue builds, so latency actually gets worse under load.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See the way that works\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-good\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The way that works\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>vllm serve model --max-num-seqs 64 --gpu-memory-utilization 0.90\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>Use continuous batching with a sensible limit, and measure latency at the load you expect.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Setting a very large max-model-len that nobody uses\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running two engines on one GPU without lowering memory utilization\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Redownloading weights on every restart\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Comparing engines with prompts that are nothing like real traffic\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"why-a-loop-is-not-enough","text":"Why a loop is not enough"},{"id":"the-engines","text":"The engines"},{"id":"a-vllm-example","text":"A vLLM example"},{"id":"operating-notes","text":"Operating notes"},{"id":"incident-the-server-that-was-slow-with-one-gpu-idle","text":"Incident: The server that was slow with one GPU idle"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"vLLM documentation","u":"https://docs.vllm.ai/en/latest/"},{"t":"Hugging Face: Text Generation Inference","u":"https://huggingface.co/docs/text-generation-inference/index"},{"t":"NVIDIA: Triton Inference Server","u":"https://docs.nvidia.com/deeplearning/triton-inference-server/user-guide/docs/index.html"}],"text":"Running a model in a notebook is easy. Serving it to many people at once, with steady latency and good use of the GPU, needs a serving engine. What you will learn Why a plain loop wastes the GPU Continuous batching and PagedAttention The main engines: vLLM, TGI, TensorRT-LLM and Triton The vLLM flags you will tune Why a loop is not enough A simple server handles one request, or a fixed batch of requests, at a time. Replies have different lengths, so a fixed batch waits for the longest one while the GPU idles for the finished ones. New requests wait until the batch ends. Continuous batching (also called in-flight batching) fixes this. After every generated token, the engine can drop finished sequences and add waiting ones, so the GPU stays full and new users start sooner. PagedAttention , used by vLLM, stores the KV cache in small fixed pages instead of one large block per request. This reduces wasted memory from fragmentation, so more requests fit in the same GPU. Prefix caching reuses the cache for a shared prompt start, such as a long system prompt. The engines vLLM : an open-source engine with continuous batching, PagedAttention and an OpenAI-compatible HTTP API. A popular default for open models. Text Generation Inference (TGI) : Hugging Face&#39;s server with similar features, tied to the Hugging Face ecosystem. TensorRT-LLM : NVIDIA&#39;s library that compiles models into optimised kernels for NVIDIA GPUs. It can be the fastest on those cards, with more setup work. Triton Inference Server : NVIDIA&#39;s general serving platform for many model types, which can host TensorRT-LLM and other backends. Hosted APIs : the provider runs the stack, and you pay per token. Choose by model support, hardware, operational skill and how much you want to tune. Many teams start with vLLM and measure. A vLLM example $ vllm serve meta-llama/Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct \\ --max-model-len 8192 \\ --gpu-memory-utilization 0.90 \\ --max-num-seqs 64 INFO Loading model weights took 14.99 GiB INFO GPU KV cache size: 41,216 tokens INFO Maximum concurrency for 8192 tokens per request: 5.03x INFO Uvicorn running on http://0.0.0.0:8000 $ curl -s localhost:8000/v1/chat/completions -H &#x27;Content-Type: application/json&#x27; \\ -d &#x27;{\"model\":\"meta-llama/Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct\",\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"hello\"}],\"max_tokens\":20}&#x27; Key settings: --max-model-len : the longest context accepted. Setting it higher makes the engine reserve for longer requests and reduces how many fit. --gpu-memory-utilization : the fraction of VRAM the engine may use, including the cache. If other processes share the GPU, lower it. --max-num-seqs : how many sequences run together. --tensor-parallel-size N : split each layer across N GPUs, needed when the model is larger than one card. It needs fast links between GPUs. The log line \"GPU KV cache size\" is the most useful number at startup. It says how many tokens of cache the memory can hold, and so how many concurrent users with how long a context. Operating notes Model weights are large (tens of GB). Cache them on a persistent volume so a restart does not download them again. Start-up takes minutes. Use readiness probes, and do not send traffic until the model is loaded. Pin the engine version, and test an upgrade, because behaviour and defaults change. Incident: The server that was slow with one GPU idle Incident A two-GPU server delivered only the speed of one. $ nvidia-smi --query-gpu=index,utilization.gpu,memory.used --format=csv,noheader 0, 98 %, 76210 MiB 1, 0 %, 4 MiB $ ps aux | grep vllm | head -1 vllm serve mistralai/Mixtral-8x7B-Instruct-v0.1 --max-model-len 16384 The engine had been started without --tensor-parallel-size 2 , so it used only the first GPU, squeezed the model and the cache into it, and left the second card empty. After the flag was added the model was split over both cards, the cache became larger and throughput roughly doubled. The monitoring that revealed it was the per-GPU utilization, w"},"gpu-serving/gpus-on-kubernetes":{"html":"\u003cp>Kubernetes does not know what a GPU is until you tell it. Once you do, GPUs are scheduled like other resources, with a few differences that cause most of the trouble.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The NVIDIA device plugin and GPU Operator\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Requesting GPUs, and why they are whole numbers\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Taints, node pools and cost\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Sharing GPUs with MIG and time-slicing\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-gpus-reach-pods\">How GPUs reach pods\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A GPU node needs three things: the NVIDIA driver, a container runtime hook that exposes devices to containers, and the \u003cstrong>device plugin\u003c/strong>, a daemonset that tells the kubelet how many GPUs the node has. The \u003cstrong>NVIDIA GPU Operator\u003c/strong> installs and manages these together. When it works, the node reports an extra resource:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl describe node gpu-node-1 | grep -A2 'nvidia.com/gpu'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl describe node gpu-node-1 | grep -A2 \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;nvidia.com/gpu&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  nvidia.com/gpu:     2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">...\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  nvidia.com/gpu     0           0\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A pod asks for GPUs in its container limits:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"resources:\n  limits:\n    nvidia.com/gpu: 1\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">resources:\n  limits:\n    nvidia.com/gpu: 1\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Extended resources such as this one must be set in \u003ccode>limits\u003c/code> (the request, if given, must equal it), and they are \u003cstrong>integers\u003c/strong>: you cannot ask for half a GPU, and they are not overcommitted. One pod holds its GPU even if it is idle.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"keeping-other-pods-away\">Keeping other pods away\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>GPU nodes are expensive. Taint them so that ordinary pods do not land there and keep the node busy:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl taint nodes gpu-node-1 nvidia.com/gpu=present:NoSchedule\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl taint nodes gpu-node-1 nvidia.com/gpu=present:NoSchedule\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>GPU pods then need a matching \u003cstrong>toleration\u003c/strong>. A \u003cstrong>node pool\u003c/strong> or node group of GPU machines that scales from zero lets you pay only while jobs run, with some start-up delay.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"when-a-pod-stays-pending\">When a pod stays Pending\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl describe pod llm-0 | tail -4\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl describe pod llm-0 | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -4\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Events:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  Warning  FailedScheduling  default-scheduler  0/6 nodes are available: 2 Insufficient nvidia.com/gpu, 4 node(s) had untolerated taint {nvidia.com/gpu: present}.\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Read this as: two nodes have GPUs but all are in use, and the other four have a taint the pod does not tolerate. The fix is to free a GPU, add capacity, or fix the toleration, depending on which line matches your intent. If no node shows \u003ccode>nvidia.com/gpu\u003c/code> in its capacity at all, the device plugin or driver is not working: check the daemonset pods and the node logs.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"sharing-a-gpu\">Sharing a GPU\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A whole GPU for a small model wastes money. Options:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>MIG (Multi-Instance GPU)\u003c/strong>: on some NVIDIA data centre cards, one GPU is split into isolated slices with their own memory and compute. Pods request a slice, such as \u003ccode>nvidia.com/mig-1g.10gb\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Time-slicing\u003c/strong>: several pods take turns on one GPU. There is no memory isolation, so one pod can exhaust memory and crash others, and latency becomes uneven.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>MPS\u003c/strong> and other sharing modes exist for specific cases.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>MIG gives predictable performance and isolation. Time-slicing is for development and light, tolerant workloads.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"multi-gpu-and-multi-node\">Multi-GPU and multi-node\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A large model needs several GPUs on \u003cstrong>the same node\u003c/strong> where possible, since links between GPUs inside a node (NVLink) are much faster than the network. A pod that asks for 4 GPUs needs a node with 4 free ones, and a cluster full of 2-GPU nodes can never schedule it. Distributed training across nodes needs fast networking and a scheduler that starts all pieces together (gang scheduling), or the job can deadlock with half its pods running.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-eight-gpus-and-a-pending-pod\">Incident: Eight GPUs and a Pending pod\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A team had eight free GPUs in the cluster and their four-GPU job stayed Pending.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get nodes -l accelerator=nvidia -o custom-columns=NAME:.metadata.name,GPU:.status.allocatable.nvidia\\.com/gpu\nkubectl describe pod trainer-0 | grep FailedScheduling\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get nodes -l accelerator=nvidia -o custom-columns=NAME:.metadata.name,GPU:.status.allocatable.nvidia\\.com/gpu\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME          GPU\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">gpu-node-1    2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">gpu-node-2    2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">gpu-node-3    2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">gpu-node-4    2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl describe pod trainer-0 | grep FailedScheduling\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">0/4 nodes are available: 4 Insufficient nvidia.com/gpu.\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The cluster did have eight free GPUs, but spread across four nodes with two each, and a pod must fit on one node. The team created a node pool of 8-GPU machines for large jobs and kept the 2-GPU nodes for small ones. The aggregate number of free GPUs was not the number that mattered.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Leaving GPU nodes untainted so system pods consume capacity\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Requesting GPUs only in requests, or asking for fractions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using time-slicing for production with strict latency\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting that drivers and the operator version must match the node image\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"how-gpus-reach-pods","text":"How GPUs reach pods"},{"id":"keeping-other-pods-away","text":"Keeping other pods away"},{"id":"when-a-pod-stays-pending","text":"When a pod stays Pending"},{"id":"sharing-a-gpu","text":"Sharing a GPU"},{"id":"multi-gpu-and-multi-node","text":"Multi-GPU and multi-node"},{"id":"incident-eight-gpus-and-a-pending-pod","text":"Incident: Eight GPUs and a Pending pod"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kubernetes: Schedule GPUs","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/manage-gpus/scheduling-gpus/"},{"t":"NVIDIA GPU Operator documentation","u":"https://docs.nvidia.com/datacenter/cloud-native/gpu-operator/latest/index.html"},{"t":"Kubernetes: Taints and tolerations","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/scheduling-eviction/taint-and-toleration/"}],"text":"Kubernetes does not know what a GPU is until you tell it. Once you do, GPUs are scheduled like other resources, with a few differences that cause most of the trouble. What you will learn The NVIDIA device plugin and GPU Operator Requesting GPUs, and why they are whole numbers Taints, node pools and cost Sharing GPUs with MIG and time-slicing How GPUs reach pods A GPU node needs three things: the NVIDIA driver, a container runtime hook that exposes devices to containers, and the device plugin , a daemonset that tells the kubelet how many GPUs the node has. The NVIDIA GPU Operator installs and manages these together. When it works, the node reports an extra resource: $ kubectl describe node gpu-node-1 | grep -A2 &#x27;nvidia.com/gpu&#x27; nvidia.com/gpu: 2 ... nvidia.com/gpu 0 0 A pod asks for GPUs in its container limits: resources: limits: nvidia.com/gpu: 1 Extended resources such as this one must be set in limits (the request, if given, must equal it), and they are integers : you cannot ask for half a GPU, and they are not overcommitted. One pod holds its GPU even if it is idle. Keeping other pods away GPU nodes are expensive. Taint them so that ordinary pods do not land there and keep the node busy: kubectl taint nodes gpu-node-1 nvidia.com/gpu=present:NoSchedule GPU pods then need a matching toleration . A node pool or node group of GPU machines that scales from zero lets you pay only while jobs run, with some start-up delay. When a pod stays Pending $ kubectl describe pod llm-0 | tail -4 Events: Warning FailedScheduling default-scheduler 0/6 nodes are available: 2 Insufficient nvidia.com/gpu, 4 node(s) had untolerated taint {nvidia.com/gpu: present}. Read this as: two nodes have GPUs but all are in use, and the other four have a taint the pod does not tolerate. The fix is to free a GPU, add capacity, or fix the toleration, depending on which line matches your intent. If no node shows nvidia.com/gpu in its capacity at all, the device plugin or driver is not working: check the daemonset pods and the node logs. Sharing a GPU A whole GPU for a small model wastes money. Options: MIG (Multi-Instance GPU) : on some NVIDIA data centre cards, one GPU is split into isolated slices with their own memory and compute. Pods request a slice, such as nvidia.com/mig-1g.10gb . Time-slicing : several pods take turns on one GPU. There is no memory isolation, so one pod can exhaust memory and crash others, and latency becomes uneven. MPS and other sharing modes exist for specific cases. MIG gives predictable performance and isolation. Time-slicing is for development and light, tolerant workloads. Multi-GPU and multi-node A large model needs several GPUs on the same node where possible, since links between GPUs inside a node (NVLink) are much faster than the network. A pod that asks for 4 GPUs needs a node with 4 free ones, and a cluster full of 2-GPU nodes can never schedule it. Distributed training across nodes needs fast networking and a scheduler that starts all pieces together (gang scheduling), or the job can deadlock with half its pods running. Incident: Eight GPUs and a Pending pod Incident A team had eight free GPUs in the cluster and their four-GPU job stayed Pending. $ kubectl get nodes -l accelerator=nvidia -o custom-columns=NAME:.metadata.name,GPU:.status.allocatable.nvidia\\.com/gpu NAME GPU gpu-node-1 2 gpu-node-2 2 gpu-node-3 2 gpu-node-4 2 $ kubectl describe pod trainer-0 | grep FailedScheduling 0/4 nodes are available: 4 Insufficient nvidia.com/gpu. The cluster did have eight free GPUs, but spread across four nodes with two each, and a pod must fit on one node. The team created a node pool of 8-GPU machines for large jobs and kept the 2-GPU nodes for small ones. The aggregate number of free GPUs was not the number that mattered. Common mistakes Leaving GPU nodes untainted so system pods consume capacity Requesting GPUs only in requests, or asking for fractions Using time-slicing for production with strict latency Forgetting tha"},"gpu-serving/scaling-latency-and-cost":{"html":"\u003cp>GPU inference costs money every minute it runs, whether or not anyone is using it. Good operation means knowing what the users experience, scaling to match demand, and being able to say what a million tokens cost.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The latency numbers: TTFT, inter-token latency and total time\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Why CPU-based autoscaling does not work for GPUs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Scaling on queue depth and concurrency\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Cost per million tokens and how utilisation changes it\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-users-feel\">What users feel\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>TTFT (time to first token)\u003c/strong>: how long before anything appears. It includes queue wait and prefill. Long prompts and busy servers raise it.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Inter-token latency (ITL)\u003c/strong>: the gap between tokens after the first. At about 20 tokens per second per user the text keeps ahead of reading speed.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Throughput\u003c/strong>: tokens per second summed across users, which sets cost.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>p95 and p99\u003c/strong>: slow outliers matter more than the average. Track percentiles.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Latency and throughput pull against each other. A bigger batch raises total throughput and slows each user a little. Set a target, such as &quot;p95 TTFT under 1.5 seconds and 20 tokens/s per user&quot;, and let it decide the batch limits.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"curl -s localhost:8000/metrics | grep -E 'vllm:(num_requests_(running|waiting)|gpu_cache_usage_perc)'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -s localhost:8000/metrics | grep -E \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;vllm:(num_requests_(running|waiting)|gpu_cache_usage_perc)&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">vllm:num_requests_running{model_name=&quot;llama-8b&quot;} 64\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">vllm:num_requests_waiting{model_name=&quot;llama-8b&quot;} 37\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">vllm:gpu_cache_usage_perc{model_name=&quot;llama-8b&quot;} 0.97\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Here 64 are running and 37 are waiting, with the cache 97 percent full. Users in the queue see high TTFT. More capacity is needed, or shorter contexts.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"scaling-on-the-right-signal\">Scaling on the right signal\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>GPU use percentages are a poor signal. A GPU can read 100 percent &quot;utilized&quot; while only partly busy in terms of throughput, and CPU on the pod says nothing. Better signals:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Queue length\u003c/strong> (requests waiting)\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Concurrent requests\u003c/strong> per replica compared with a tested maximum\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>KV cache usage\u003c/strong>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>TTFT or latency percentiles\u003c/strong> as a guard\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>With KEDA or the Horizontal Pod Autoscaler on custom metrics, add replicas when the waiting count stays above a small number for a minute. Scale down slowly: a new replica needs minutes to schedule a GPU node, pull an image and load weights. Keep a minimum of one or two warm replicas for latency, and consider scaling a batch workload to zero.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cost\">Cost\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Cost per token = GPU price per hour ÷ tokens generated per hour.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>If a GPU costs 4 USD per hour and serves 2 500 tokens per second at full load, it produces 9 million tokens per hour, so about 0.44 USD per million tokens. If it only runs at 25 percent of that, the cost per million tokens is four times higher, about 1.78 USD. \u003cstrong>Utilisation\u003c/strong> is the biggest lever. The ways to improve it are:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Larger batches and continuous batching\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Quantization, so more fits and faster decode\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Smaller models for easy tasks, with routing to bigger ones when needed\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Prefix caching for repeated prompts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Spot or preemptible GPUs for batch work that can restart, with checkpoints\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reserved capacity for the stable base load\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Compare against a hosted API price per million tokens at your real volume. Hosted wins at low or spiky use; self-hosting wins at steady high use, or when privacy and control matter.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-scaling-on-the-wrong-metric\">Incident: Scaling on the wrong metric\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A launch-day traffic spike produced timeouts although autoscaling was enabled and CPU was low.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get hpa llm\ncurl -s llm-0:8000/metrics | grep num_requests_waiting\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get hpa llm\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME  REFERENCE         TARGETS   MINPODS  MAXPODS  REPLICAS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">llm   Deployment/llm    12%/60%   2        10       2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -s llm-0:8000/metrics | grep num_requests_waiting\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">vllm:num_requests_waiting{model_name=&quot;llama-8b&quot;} 211\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The autoscaler used CPU, which stays low when the heavy work happens on the GPU, so it saw nothing wrong while 211 requests waited. They switched to scaling on waiting requests per replica, with a rule of 10, and kept four replicas warm for events. New replicas still took six minutes to start, so a model image with weights baked in and a node pool with a spare node cut that to two. The metric that you scale on must move when users suffer.\u003c/p>\n\u003csection class=\"wp\" aria-label=\"Worked example with a wrong path\">\u003ch4 class=\"wp-h\">Worked example: a tempting wrong path\u003c/h4>\u003cp class=\"wp-goal\">\u003cstrong>Goal.\u003c/strong> Autoscale the model service on CPU like any other service.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-bad\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The tempting way\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>metrics:\n- type: Resource\n  resource: {name: cpu, target: {type: Utilization, averageUtilization: 70}}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See what goes wrong\u003c/summary>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>(CPU stays at 15%)\n$ kubectl get hpa\nreplicas 2/2\ntime to first token: 11 s\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp class=\"wp-why\">\u003cstrong>Why it fails.\u003c/strong> The work is done on the GPU, so CPU says little about load. The queue grows while the autoscaler sees no reason to act.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See the way that works\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-good\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The way that works\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>metrics:\n- type: Pods\n  pods:\n    metric: {name: vllm:num_requests_waiting}\n    target: {type: AverageValue, averageValue: &quot;4&quot;}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>Scale on waiting requests or time to first token, and keep warm replicas because model loading is slow.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Scaling GPU pods on CPU\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Averaging latency and ignoring p95\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Scaling down fast and paying start-up time again\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Comparing GPU cost with API cost at a single quiet hour\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"what-users-feel","text":"What users feel"},{"id":"scaling-on-the-right-signal","text":"Scaling on the right signal"},{"id":"cost","text":"Cost"},{"id":"incident-scaling-on-the-wrong-metric","text":"Incident: Scaling on the wrong metric"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kubernetes: Horizontal Pod Autoscaling","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/run-application/horizontal-pod-autoscale/"},{"t":"KEDA documentation","u":"https://keda.sh/docs/"},{"t":"vLLM: Metrics","u":"https://docs.vllm.ai/en/latest/serving/metrics.html"}],"text":"GPU inference costs money every minute it runs, whether or not anyone is using it. Good operation means knowing what the users experience, scaling to match demand, and being able to say what a million tokens cost. What you will learn The latency numbers: TTFT, inter-token latency and total time Why CPU-based autoscaling does not work for GPUs Scaling on queue depth and concurrency Cost per million tokens and how utilisation changes it What users feel TTFT (time to first token) : how long before anything appears. It includes queue wait and prefill. Long prompts and busy servers raise it. Inter-token latency (ITL) : the gap between tokens after the first. At about 20 tokens per second per user the text keeps ahead of reading speed. Throughput : tokens per second summed across users, which sets cost. p95 and p99 : slow outliers matter more than the average. Track percentiles. Latency and throughput pull against each other. A bigger batch raises total throughput and slows each user a little. Set a target, such as \"p95 TTFT under 1.5 seconds and 20 tokens/s per user\", and let it decide the batch limits. $ curl -s localhost:8000/metrics | grep -E &#x27;vllm:(num_requests_(running|waiting)|gpu_cache_usage_perc)&#x27; vllm:num_requests_running{model_name=\"llama-8b\"} 64 vllm:num_requests_waiting{model_name=\"llama-8b\"} 37 vllm:gpu_cache_usage_perc{model_name=\"llama-8b\"} 0.97 Here 64 are running and 37 are waiting, with the cache 97 percent full. Users in the queue see high TTFT. More capacity is needed, or shorter contexts. Scaling on the right signal GPU use percentages are a poor signal. A GPU can read 100 percent \"utilized\" while only partly busy in terms of throughput, and CPU on the pod says nothing. Better signals: Queue length (requests waiting) Concurrent requests per replica compared with a tested maximum KV cache usage TTFT or latency percentiles as a guard With KEDA or the Horizontal Pod Autoscaler on custom metrics, add replicas when the waiting count stays above a small number for a minute. Scale down slowly: a new replica needs minutes to schedule a GPU node, pull an image and load weights. Keep a minimum of one or two warm replicas for latency, and consider scaling a batch workload to zero. Cost Cost per token = GPU price per hour ÷ tokens generated per hour. If a GPU costs 4 USD per hour and serves 2 500 tokens per second at full load, it produces 9 million tokens per hour, so about 0.44 USD per million tokens. If it only runs at 25 percent of that, the cost per million tokens is four times higher, about 1.78 USD. Utilisation is the biggest lever. The ways to improve it are: Larger batches and continuous batching Quantization, so more fits and faster decode Smaller models for easy tasks, with routing to bigger ones when needed Prefix caching for repeated prompts Spot or preemptible GPUs for batch work that can restart, with checkpoints Reserved capacity for the stable base load Compare against a hosted API price per million tokens at your real volume. Hosted wins at low or spiky use; self-hosting wins at steady high use, or when privacy and control matter. Incident: Scaling on the wrong metric Incident A launch-day traffic spike produced timeouts although autoscaling was enabled and CPU was low. $ kubectl get hpa llm NAME REFERENCE TARGETS MINPODS MAXPODS REPLICAS llm Deployment/llm 12%/60% 2 10 2 $ curl -s llm-0:8000/metrics | grep num_requests_waiting vllm:num_requests_waiting{model_name=\"llama-8b\"} 211 The autoscaler used CPU, which stays low when the heavy work happens on the GPU, so it saw nothing wrong while 211 requests waited. They switched to scaling on waiting requests per replica, with a rule of 10, and kept four replicas warm for events. New replicas still took six minutes to start, so a model image with weights baked in and a node pool with a spare node cut that to two. The metric that you scale on must move when users suffer. Worked example: a tempting wrong path Goal. Autoscale the model service on CPU lik"},"gpu-serving/monitoring-and-debugging-gpu-workloads":{"html":"\u003cp>GPU problems look like application problems until you look at the GPU. A few tools and patterns find most of them.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Reading nvidia-smi\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>DCGM and the metrics worth alerting on\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The usual faults: out of memory, throttling, XID errors, hangs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A debugging order\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"nvidia-smi\">nvidia-smi\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"nvidia-smi\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>nvidia-smi\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">| GPU  Name              Persistence-M | Bus-Id       Disp.A | Volatile Uncorr. ECC |\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">| Fan  Temp   Perf       Pwr:Usage/Cap |         Memory-Usage | GPU-Util  Compute M. |\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">|   0  NVIDIA A100-SXM4-80GB        Off | 00000000:07:00.0 Off |                    0 |\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">| N/A   71C    P0     392W / 400W |  78410MiB / 81920MiB |     99%      Default |\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">| Processes:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">|  GPU  PID    Type  Process name                              GPU Memory Usage |\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">|    0  18211  C     /usr/bin/python3                                  78120MiB |\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Memory-Usage\u003c/strong> is the first thing to check for out-of-memory problems. The \u003cstrong>Processes\u003c/strong> list says which program holds it.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>GPU-Util\u003c/strong> is the share of time at least one kernel was running. It is not a measure of how efficient the work is.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Temp\u003c/strong> and \u003cstrong>Pwr\u003c/strong> near the cap can lead to throttling.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>ECC errors\u003c/strong> (the &quot;Volatile Uncorr. ECC&quot; column) above zero point to a failing memory.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>nvidia-smi dmon\u003c/code> streams utilization, and \u003ccode>nvidia-smi -q\u003c/code> prints everything.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"dcgm-and-metrics\">DCGM and metrics\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>In Kubernetes, the \u003cstrong>DCGM exporter\u003c/strong> publishes GPU metrics for Prometheus. Useful ones include GPU utilization, framebuffer memory used, temperature, power, SM clock and XID errors. Alert on:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Memory used close to total for several minutes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Temperature or power at limits with clocks dropping (throttling)\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Any XID error or growing ECC error count\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Utilization near zero on a pod that holds a GPU (an idle reservation that costs money)\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-faults\">Common faults\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Out of memory (OOM).\u003c/strong> The message names the sizes:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">torch.OutOfMemoryError: CUDA out of memory. Tried to allocate 2.00 GiB.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">GPU 0 has a total capacity of 79.15 GiB of which 1.12 GiB is free.\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Check which process holds the memory (another model on the same card is common), reduce the batch size or context length, lower \u003ccode>--gpu-memory-utilization\u003c/code>, quantize, or split across GPUs. A leftover process from a crashed job can hold memory: find it in \u003ccode>nvidia-smi\u003c/code> and stop it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Throttling.\u003c/strong> The GPU lowers its clocks when hot or at a power limit, and speed drops without an error. Check temperature, fan or cooling, and clock values. A card that is slow only in the afternoon often points to cooling.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>XID errors.\u003c/strong> The driver writes them to the kernel log:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"dmesg | grep -i xid\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>dmesg | grep -i xid\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NVRM: Xid (PCI:0000:07:00): 79, pid=18211, GPU has fallen off the bus.\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>XID 79 means the GPU has fallen off the bus: usually a hardware, power or seating fault. Drain the node, move the work, and have the hardware checked. Other codes have their own meanings in NVIDIA&#39;s documentation; look each one up.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Hung multi-GPU jobs.\u003c/strong> Training or tensor-parallel serving uses NCCL for communication. If one rank dies or the network between nodes fails, the others wait forever and show 100 percent utilization while doing nothing. Set \u003ccode>NCCL_DEBUG=INFO\u003c/code> to see where it stops, set timeouts, and check that all ranks started and the network interfaces are right.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Driver and CUDA mismatches.\u003c/strong> A container&#39;s CUDA version must be supported by the host driver. Errors such as &quot;CUDA driver version is insufficient for CUDA runtime version&quot; mean the driver needs updating or the image needs an older CUDA.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"debugging-order\">Debugging order\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Is the GPU visible? \u003ccode>nvidia-smi\u003c/code>, then inside the container.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Is memory full, and who holds it?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Are temperature, power and clocks normal?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Does the kernel log show XID errors?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>For multi-GPU work, did every rank start and can they talk?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Do driver and library versions match?\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-job-that-ran-at-100-percent-and-did-nothing\">Incident: The job that ran at 100 percent and did nothing\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A four-GPU training job showed full utilization for six hours and no progress.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl logs trainer-2 | tail -2\nkubectl get pods -l job=train -o wide | grep -c Running\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl logs trainer-2 | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">[rank2] NCCL WARN Net : Connect to 10.0.4.17&lt;45214&gt; failed : Connection timed out\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get pods -l job=train -o wide | grep -c Running\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">3\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Only three of four workers were running. The fourth had been evicted from its node, and the other three waited for it inside a collective operation, which keeps the GPU shown as busy. Utilization was a misleading signal. The team added a job-level timeout so the group failed fast, restarts from checkpoints, and alerts when the number of running workers is below the expected count. A busy GPU is not the same as progress.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using GPU-Util as a measure of useful work\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring the kernel log for XID errors\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running two memory-hungry processes on one card\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No timeouts on collective operations\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"nvidia-smi","text":"nvidia-smi"},{"id":"dcgm-and-metrics","text":"DCGM and metrics"},{"id":"common-faults","text":"Common faults"},{"id":"debugging-order","text":"Debugging order"},{"id":"incident-the-job-that-ran-at-100-percent-and-did-nothing","text":"Incident: The job that ran at 100 percent and did nothing"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"NVIDIA: nvidia-smi","u":"https://docs.nvidia.com/deploy/nvidia-smi/index.html"},{"t":"NVIDIA: DCGM","u":"https://docs.nvidia.com/datacenter/dcgm/latest/index.html"},{"t":"NVIDIA: XID errors","u":"https://docs.nvidia.com/deploy/xid-errors/index.html"}],"text":"GPU problems look like application problems until you look at the GPU. A few tools and patterns find most of them. What you will learn Reading nvidia-smi DCGM and the metrics worth alerting on The usual faults: out of memory, throttling, XID errors, hangs A debugging order nvidia-smi $ nvidia-smi | GPU Name Persistence-M | Bus-Id Disp.A | Volatile Uncorr. ECC | | Fan Temp Perf Pwr:Usage/Cap | Memory-Usage | GPU-Util Compute M. | | 0 NVIDIA A100-SXM4-80GB Off | 00000000:07:00.0 Off | 0 | | N/A 71C P0 392W / 400W | 78410MiB / 81920MiB | 99% Default | | Processes: | GPU PID Type Process name GPU Memory Usage | | 0 18211 C /usr/bin/python3 78120MiB | Memory-Usage is the first thing to check for out-of-memory problems. The Processes list says which program holds it. GPU-Util is the share of time at least one kernel was running. It is not a measure of how efficient the work is. Temp and Pwr near the cap can lead to throttling. ECC errors (the \"Volatile Uncorr. ECC\" column) above zero point to a failing memory. nvidia-smi dmon streams utilization, and nvidia-smi -q prints everything. DCGM and metrics In Kubernetes, the DCGM exporter publishes GPU metrics for Prometheus. Useful ones include GPU utilization, framebuffer memory used, temperature, power, SM clock and XID errors. Alert on: Memory used close to total for several minutes Temperature or power at limits with clocks dropping (throttling) Any XID error or growing ECC error count Utilization near zero on a pod that holds a GPU (an idle reservation that costs money) Common faults Out of memory (OOM). The message names the sizes: torch.OutOfMemoryError: CUDA out of memory. Tried to allocate 2.00 GiB. GPU 0 has a total capacity of 79.15 GiB of which 1.12 GiB is free. Check which process holds the memory (another model on the same card is common), reduce the batch size or context length, lower --gpu-memory-utilization , quantize, or split across GPUs. A leftover process from a crashed job can hold memory: find it in nvidia-smi and stop it. Throttling. The GPU lowers its clocks when hot or at a power limit, and speed drops without an error. Check temperature, fan or cooling, and clock values. A card that is slow only in the afternoon often points to cooling. XID errors. The driver writes them to the kernel log: $ dmesg | grep -i xid NVRM: Xid (PCI:0000:07:00): 79, pid=18211, GPU has fallen off the bus. XID 79 means the GPU has fallen off the bus: usually a hardware, power or seating fault. Drain the node, move the work, and have the hardware checked. Other codes have their own meanings in NVIDIA&#39;s documentation; look each one up. Hung multi-GPU jobs. Training or tensor-parallel serving uses NCCL for communication. If one rank dies or the network between nodes fails, the others wait forever and show 100 percent utilization while doing nothing. Set NCCL_DEBUG=INFO to see where it stops, set timeouts, and check that all ranks started and the network interfaces are right. Driver and CUDA mismatches. A container&#39;s CUDA version must be supported by the host driver. Errors such as \"CUDA driver version is insufficient for CUDA runtime version\" mean the driver needs updating or the image needs an older CUDA. Debugging order Is the GPU visible? nvidia-smi , then inside the container. Is memory full, and who holds it? Are temperature, power and clocks normal? Does the kernel log show XID errors? For multi-GPU work, did every rank start and can they talk? Do driver and library versions match? Incident: The job that ran at 100 percent and did nothing Incident A four-GPU training job showed full utilization for six hours and no progress. $ kubectl logs trainer-2 | tail -2 [rank2] NCCL WARN Net : Connect to 10.0.4.17\u003c45214> failed : Connection timed out $ kubectl get pods -l job=train -o wide | grep -c Running 3 Only three of four workers were running. The fourth had been evicted from its node, and the other three waited for it inside a collective operation, which keeps the GPU shown as bus"},"gpu-serving/quantization-and-model-sizing":{"html":"\u003cp>Before choosing a GPU, you need to know how much memory the model will take. Most surprises come from forgetting the cache and overhead. Quantization is the main lever for fitting larger models into smaller, cheaper hardware.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Calculating memory for weights\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Why the KV cache and overhead matter\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Common quantization formats and trade-offs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Testing quality before you commit\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-memory-math\">The memory math\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"def weights_gb(params_billion, bytes_per_param):\n    return params_billion * bytes_per_param\n\nfor name, b in [(&quot;fp16&quot;, 2.0), (&quot;int8&quot;, 1.0), (&quot;int4&quot;, 0.5)]:\n    print(name, weights_gb(70, b), &quot;GB for a 70B model&quot;)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">weights_gb\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">params_billion, bytes_per_param\u003c/span>):\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> params_billion * bytes_per_param\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> name, b \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> [(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;fp16&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2.0\u003c/span>), (\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;int8&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1.0\u003c/span>), (\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;int4&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0.5\u003c/span>)]:\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(name, weights_gb(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">70\u003c/span>, b), \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;GB for a 70B model&quot;\u003c/span>)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Weights for a 70B model are about 140 GB in FP16, 70 GB in INT8 and 35 GB at 4 bits. Add the \u003cstrong>KV cache\u003c/strong>, which grows with context length and concurrent requests, plus activations and runtime overhead. A rule of thumb is to keep 10 to 20 percent of GPU memory free and to budget the cache explicitly.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"formats\">Formats\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>FP16 or BF16:\u003c/strong> the usual baseline, best quality, largest size.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>FP8 and INT8:\u003c/strong> about half the memory with small quality loss on modern hardware.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>4-bit (AWQ, GPTQ, GGUF variants):\u003c/strong> large savings, more quality risk, especially for small models and long reasoning.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Lower precision also reduces memory traffic, which often improves token rate, because decoding is limited by memory bandwidth.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"test-before-you-ship\">Test before you ship\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# serve a quantized model and compare with the baseline on your own prompts\nvllm serve ./llama-70b-awq --quantization awq --max-model-len 8192 --gpu-memory-utilization 0.90\npython eval_prompts.py --endpoint http://localhost:8000/v1 --set support-tickets.jsonl\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># serve a quantized model and compare with the baseline on your own prompts\u003c/span>\nvllm serve ./llama-70b-awq --quantization awq --max-model-len 8192 --gpu-memory-utilization 0.90\npython eval_prompts.py --endpoint http://localhost:8000/v1 --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">set\u003c/span> support-tickets.jsonl\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Use a task-specific evaluation set, not only public benchmarks. Compare accuracy, refusal behavior and formatting, and measure latency and throughput. Record the chosen format with the model version so everyone serves the same thing.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-out-of-memory-on-load\">Incident: Out of memory on load\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A 70B model in FP16 could not load on one 80 GB GPU.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">torch.cuda.OutOfMemoryError: CUDA out of memory.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Tried to allocate 1.88 GiB\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">GPU 0 capacity 79.2 GiB, 1.1 GiB free\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The team had counted only parameters and assumed it would fit. In FP16 the weights alone needed about 140 GB. They moved to a 4-bit format, reserved cache memory in the plan and verified quality on their ticket dataset before rollout.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Counting weights but not the KV cache\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choosing a format using only public benchmarks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Filling GPU memory to 100 percent\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not recording which quantization is deployed\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-memory-math","text":"The memory math"},{"id":"formats","text":"Formats"},{"id":"test-before-you-ship","text":"Test before you ship"},{"id":"incident-out-of-memory-on-load","text":"Incident: Out of memory on load"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"NVIDIA developer documentation","u":"https://docs.nvidia.com/"},{"t":"Hugging Face quantization guide","u":"https://huggingface.co/docs/transformers/main/en/quantization/overview"},{"t":"vLLM quantization","u":"https://docs.vllm.ai/en/latest/features/quantization/index.html"}],"text":"Before choosing a GPU, you need to know how much memory the model will take. Most surprises come from forgetting the cache and overhead. Quantization is the main lever for fitting larger models into smaller, cheaper hardware. What you will learn Calculating memory for weights Why the KV cache and overhead matter Common quantization formats and trade-offs Testing quality before you commit The memory math def weights_gb ( params_billion, bytes_per_param ): return params_billion * bytes_per_param for name, b in [( \"fp16\" , 2.0 ), ( \"int8\" , 1.0 ), ( \"int4\" , 0.5 )]: print (name, weights_gb( 70 , b), \"GB for a 70B model\" ) Weights for a 70B model are about 140 GB in FP16, 70 GB in INT8 and 35 GB at 4 bits. Add the KV cache , which grows with context length and concurrent requests, plus activations and runtime overhead. A rule of thumb is to keep 10 to 20 percent of GPU memory free and to budget the cache explicitly. Formats FP16 or BF16: the usual baseline, best quality, largest size. FP8 and INT8: about half the memory with small quality loss on modern hardware. 4-bit (AWQ, GPTQ, GGUF variants): large savings, more quality risk, especially for small models and long reasoning. Lower precision also reduces memory traffic, which often improves token rate, because decoding is limited by memory bandwidth. Test before you ship # serve a quantized model and compare with the baseline on your own prompts vllm serve ./llama-70b-awq --quantization awq --max-model-len 8192 --gpu-memory-utilization 0.90 python eval_prompts.py --endpoint http://localhost:8000/v1 -- set support-tickets.jsonl Use a task-specific evaluation set, not only public benchmarks. Compare accuracy, refusal behavior and formatting, and measure latency and throughput. Record the chosen format with the model version so everyone serves the same thing. Incident: Out of memory on load Incident A 70B model in FP16 could not load on one 80 GB GPU. torch.cuda.OutOfMemoryError: CUDA out of memory. Tried to allocate 1.88 GiB GPU 0 capacity 79.2 GiB, 1.1 GiB free The team had counted only parameters and assumed it would fit. In FP16 the weights alone needed about 140 GB. They moved to a 4-bit format, reserved cache memory in the plan and verified quality on their ticket dataset before rollout. Common mistakes Counting weights but not the KV cache Choosing a format using only public benchmarks Filling GPU memory to 100 percent Not recording which quantization is deployed"},"gpu-serving/batching-kv-cache-and-throughput":{"html":"\u003cp>Serving a language model is not one calculation per request. Each user needs a long series of small steps, and the GPU wastes most of its power if it does them alone. Batching and cache management are what make serving affordable.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Prefill versus decode and why they behave differently\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Continuous batching and paged KV cache\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The main limits and what they trade\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reading throughput and latency metrics\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"two-phases\">Two phases\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Prefill\u003c/strong> reads the whole prompt in one parallel pass and is compute heavy. It determines the \u003cstrong>time to first token\u003c/strong>. \u003cstrong>Decode\u003c/strong> produces one token per step, reuses the KV cache and is limited by memory bandwidth. It determines \u003cstrong>inter-token latency\u003c/strong>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"continuous-batching\">Continuous batching\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Static batching waits for a full batch and runs until the longest request ends. \u003cstrong>Continuous batching\u003c/strong> adds new requests and removes finished ones at every step, so the GPU stays busy. \u003cstrong>Paged attention\u003c/strong> stores the KV cache in small blocks, reducing waste and allowing more concurrent sequences.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"vllm serve meta/model-8b \\\n  --max-num-seqs 128 \\\n  --max-num-batched-tokens 16384 \\\n  --max-model-len 8192 \\\n  --gpu-memory-utilization 0.90\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">vllm serve meta/model-8b \\\n  --max-num-seqs 128 \\\n  --max-num-batched-tokens 16384 \\\n  --max-model-len 8192 \\\n  --gpu-memory-utilization 0.90\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-knobs\">The knobs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>max-num-seqs:\u003c/strong> more concurrent requests means higher throughput but more cache use and higher latency per request.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>max-num-batched-tokens:\u003c/strong> large values favor throughput, small ones favor smooth latency.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>max-model-len:\u003c/strong> a smaller limit leaves memory for more sequences.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Prefix caching:\u003c/strong> reuses cache for shared prompt starts, such as a system prompt.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"measure\">Measure\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"curl -s localhost:8000/metrics | grep -E &quot;num_requests_(running|waiting)|time_to_first_token|gpu_cache_usage&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">curl -s localhost:8000/metrics | grep -E \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;num_requests_(running|waiting)|time_to_first_token|gpu_cache_usage&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Watch queue length, cache usage, time to first token and tokens per second under realistic concurrency. Rising waiting requests with high cache usage means you need more memory, smaller limits or more replicas.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-fast-alone-slow-together\">Incident: Fast alone, slow together\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Latency jumped when concurrency increased because the cache was full.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">num_requests_running: 64\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">num_requests_waiting: 211\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">gpu_cache_usage_perc: 0.99\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">time_to_first_token p95: 9.4s\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The server accepted long contexts from every request and exhausted cache memory, so new requests waited. The team capped context length for the public route, enabled prefix caching for the shared system prompt and added a replica with autoscaling on queue depth.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Tuning with a single test request\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Allowing very long contexts for every route\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring queue depth and cache usage\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Setting memory utilization to 100 percent\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"two-phases","text":"Two phases"},{"id":"continuous-batching","text":"Continuous batching"},{"id":"the-knobs","text":"The knobs"},{"id":"measure","text":"Measure"},{"id":"incident-fast-alone-slow-together","text":"Incident: Fast alone, slow together"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"vLLM documentation","u":"https://docs.vllm.ai/en/latest/"},{"t":"NVIDIA Triton Inference Server","u":"https://docs.nvidia.com/deeplearning/triton-inference-server/"}],"text":"Serving a language model is not one calculation per request. Each user needs a long series of small steps, and the GPU wastes most of its power if it does them alone. Batching and cache management are what make serving affordable. What you will learn Prefill versus decode and why they behave differently Continuous batching and paged KV cache The main limits and what they trade Reading throughput and latency metrics Two phases Prefill reads the whole prompt in one parallel pass and is compute heavy. It determines the time to first token . Decode produces one token per step, reuses the KV cache and is limited by memory bandwidth. It determines inter-token latency . Continuous batching Static batching waits for a full batch and runs until the longest request ends. Continuous batching adds new requests and removes finished ones at every step, so the GPU stays busy. Paged attention stores the KV cache in small blocks, reducing waste and allowing more concurrent sequences. vllm serve meta/model-8b \\ --max-num-seqs 128 \\ --max-num-batched-tokens 16384 \\ --max-model-len 8192 \\ --gpu-memory-utilization 0.90 The knobs max-num-seqs: more concurrent requests means higher throughput but more cache use and higher latency per request. max-num-batched-tokens: large values favor throughput, small ones favor smooth latency. max-model-len: a smaller limit leaves memory for more sequences. Prefix caching: reuses cache for shared prompt starts, such as a system prompt. Measure curl -s localhost:8000/metrics | grep -E \"num_requests_(running|waiting)|time_to_first_token|gpu_cache_usage\" Watch queue length, cache usage, time to first token and tokens per second under realistic concurrency. Rising waiting requests with high cache usage means you need more memory, smaller limits or more replicas. Incident: Fast alone, slow together Incident Latency jumped when concurrency increased because the cache was full. num_requests_running: 64 num_requests_waiting: 211 gpu_cache_usage_perc: 0.99 time_to_first_token p95: 9.4s The server accepted long contexts from every request and exhausted cache memory, so new requests waited. The team capped context length for the public route, enabled prefix caching for the shared system prompt and added a replica with autoscaling on queue depth. Common mistakes Tuning with a single test request Allowing very long contexts for every route Ignoring queue depth and cache usage Setting memory utilization to 100 percent"},"gpu-serving/multi-gpu-tensor-and-pipeline-parallelism":{"html":"\u003cp>Large models need more memory than one GPU has. Splitting them works, but every split adds communication, so how and where you split changes speed dramatically.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Tensor versus pipeline parallelism\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Why interconnect bandwidth matters\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reading the GPU topology\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>When to scale with replicas instead\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"two-ways-to-split\">Two ways to split\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Tensor parallelism (TP):\u003c/strong> every layer&#39;s weights are divided across GPUs, and GPUs exchange results at each layer. It reduces latency for a single request but needs very fast links.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Pipeline parallelism (PP):\u003c/strong> each GPU holds a block of layers, and activations flow from stage to stage. Communication is lighter, but stages can sit idle without enough concurrent requests.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"nvidia-smi topo -m\nvllm serve meta/model-70b \\\n  --tensor-parallel-size 4 \\\n  --max-model-len 8192\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">nvidia-smi topo -m\nvllm serve meta/model-70b \\\n  --tensor-parallel-size 4 \\\n  --max-model-len 8192\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"topology\">Topology\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>nvidia-smi topo -m\u003c/code> shows how GPUs connect. \u003cstrong>NV#\u003c/strong> entries mean NVLink, which is fast. \u003cstrong>PIX\u003c/strong> or \u003cstrong>PHB\u003c/strong> mean paths through PCIe, which is slower. \u003cstrong>SYS\u003c/strong> crosses CPU sockets and is slowest. Choose GPUs that share fast links for the tensor-parallel group, and keep the group inside one node when possible.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"replicas-versus-splitting\">Replicas versus splitting\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>If a model fits on one GPU, run several independent replicas behind a load balancer. This gives near-linear throughput, no communication overhead and isolated failures. Split only when memory requires it, and then replicate the split group.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get pods -l app=llm -o wide\nkubectl describe node gpu-node-3 | grep -A5 &quot;nvidia.com/gpu&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl get pods -l app=llm -o wide\nkubectl describe node gpu-node-3 | grep -A5 \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;nvidia.com/gpu&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Check that all pods of one tensor-parallel group are scheduled on the same node, using node affinity or a multi-GPU resource request.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-slower-with-more-gpus\">Incident: Slower with more GPUs\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Adding GPUs across nodes made serving slower.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NCCL INFO Channel 00 via NET/Socket\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">tokens/s: 21 (single GPU: 34)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">allreduce latency: high\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The tensor-parallel group spanned two machines connected by regular Ethernet, so each layer waited on the network. The team kept tensor parallelism within a node with NVLink, used replicas to scale out and added a scheduling rule that keeps each group on one node.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Tensor parallelism across slow links\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Splitting a model that fits on one GPU\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring topology when placing pods\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using an odd GPU count that does not divide the model heads\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"two-ways-to-split","text":"Two ways to split"},{"id":"topology","text":"Topology"},{"id":"replicas-versus-splitting","text":"Replicas versus splitting"},{"id":"incident-slower-with-more-gpus","text":"Incident: Slower with more GPUs"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"vLLM parallelism and scaling","u":"https://docs.vllm.ai/en/latest/serving/distributed_serving.html"},{"t":"NVIDIA developer documentation","u":"https://docs.nvidia.com/"}],"text":"Large models need more memory than one GPU has. Splitting them works, but every split adds communication, so how and where you split changes speed dramatically. What you will learn Tensor versus pipeline parallelism Why interconnect bandwidth matters Reading the GPU topology When to scale with replicas instead Two ways to split Tensor parallelism (TP): every layer&#39;s weights are divided across GPUs, and GPUs exchange results at each layer. It reduces latency for a single request but needs very fast links. Pipeline parallelism (PP): each GPU holds a block of layers, and activations flow from stage to stage. Communication is lighter, but stages can sit idle without enough concurrent requests. nvidia-smi topo -m vllm serve meta/model-70b \\ --tensor-parallel-size 4 \\ --max-model-len 8192 Topology nvidia-smi topo -m shows how GPUs connect. NV# entries mean NVLink, which is fast. PIX or PHB mean paths through PCIe, which is slower. SYS crosses CPU sockets and is slowest. Choose GPUs that share fast links for the tensor-parallel group, and keep the group inside one node when possible. Replicas versus splitting If a model fits on one GPU, run several independent replicas behind a load balancer. This gives near-linear throughput, no communication overhead and isolated failures. Split only when memory requires it, and then replicate the split group. kubectl get pods -l app=llm -o wide kubectl describe node gpu-node-3 | grep -A5 \"nvidia.com/gpu\" Check that all pods of one tensor-parallel group are scheduled on the same node, using node affinity or a multi-GPU resource request. Incident: Slower with more GPUs Incident Adding GPUs across nodes made serving slower. NCCL INFO Channel 00 via NET/Socket tokens/s: 21 (single GPU: 34) allreduce latency: high The tensor-parallel group spanned two machines connected by regular Ethernet, so each layer waited on the network. The team kept tensor parallelism within a node with NVLink, used replicas to scale out and added a scheduling rule that keeps each group on one node. Common mistakes Tensor parallelism across slow links Splitting a model that fits on one GPU Ignoring topology when placing pods Using an odd GPU count that does not divide the model heads"},"gpu-serving/autoscaling-llm-inference":{"html":"\u003cp>GPU nodes are expensive and slow to start, so autoscaling inference is harder than scaling a web service. The right signal, warm capacity and sensible limits make the difference between smooth scaling and timeouts.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Choosing a scaling signal for model servers\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Why cold start dominates scale-up time\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Configuring KEDA or an HPA on custom metrics\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Controlling cost with limits and schedules\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-signal\">The signal\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Scale on what users feel. Good signals are \u003cstrong>queue depth\u003c/strong>, \u003cstrong>running requests per replica\u003c/strong> and \u003cstrong>time to first token\u003c/strong>. GPU utilization alone can look high while a replica is perfectly healthy, and CPU says almost nothing.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: keda.sh/v1alpha1\nkind: ScaledObject\nmetadata:\n  name: llm-scaler\nspec:\n  scaleTargetRef:\n    name: llm-server\n  minReplicaCount: 2\n  maxReplicaCount: 12\n  cooldownPeriod: 600\n  triggers:\n    - type: prometheus\n      metadata:\n        serverAddress: http://prometheus.monitoring:9090\n        query: sum(vllm:num_requests_waiting)\n        threshold: &quot;8&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">keda.sh/v1alpha1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ScaledObject\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">llm-scaler\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">scaleTargetRef:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">llm-server\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">minReplicaCount:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">maxReplicaCount:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">12\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">cooldownPeriod:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">600\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">triggers:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">type:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">prometheus\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">serverAddress:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">http://prometheus.monitoring:9090\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">query:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">sum(vllm:num_requests_waiting)\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">threshold:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;8&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cold-start\">Cold start\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Adding capacity needs a new GPU node, then an image pull, then a model download and weight load. That can take minutes. Reduce it with pre-pulled images, models on a fast local or shared volume, a pool of warm spare replicas and cluster autoscaler or Karpenter settings that keep GPU nodes ready.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cost-control\">Cost control\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Set a \u003cstrong>maximum replica count\u003c/strong> and a budget alert. Use a long scale-down delay so replicas are not removed during short dips, because reloading is costly. For predictable peaks, scale up on a schedule before the peak:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get scaledobject,hpa -n ai\nkubectl get nodes -l node.kubernetes.io/instance-type=gpu -o wide\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl get scaledobject,hpa -n ai\nkubectl get nodes -l node.kubernetes.io/instance-type=gpu -o wide\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Review the cost per thousand requests monthly.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-ten-minutes-of-timeouts\">Incident: Ten minutes of timeouts\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A traffic spike outran GPU node startup.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">09:00 queue depth 340\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">09:01 scale up 2 -&gt; 8\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">09:09 replicas ready\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">429 rate 41% until then\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Scaling worked, but new replicas needed minutes to start. The team kept a warm pool, moved model files to a local cache, added a scheduled scale-up before the known morning peak and rate-limited low-priority traffic during spikes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Scaling on CPU\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No warm capacity for slow-starting models\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Aggressive scale-down that causes reloads\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No maximum replicas or budget alert\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-signal","text":"The signal"},{"id":"cold-start","text":"Cold start"},{"id":"cost-control","text":"Cost control"},{"id":"incident-ten-minutes-of-timeouts","text":"Incident: Ten minutes of timeouts"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"KEDA documentation","u":"https://keda.sh/docs/"},{"t":"Kubernetes horizontal pod autoscaling","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/run-application/horizontal-pod-autoscale/"}],"text":"GPU nodes are expensive and slow to start, so autoscaling inference is harder than scaling a web service. The right signal, warm capacity and sensible limits make the difference between smooth scaling and timeouts. What you will learn Choosing a scaling signal for model servers Why cold start dominates scale-up time Configuring KEDA or an HPA on custom metrics Controlling cost with limits and schedules The signal Scale on what users feel. Good signals are queue depth , running requests per replica and time to first token . GPU utilization alone can look high while a replica is perfectly healthy, and CPU says almost nothing. apiVersion: keda.sh/v1alpha1 kind: ScaledObject metadata: name: llm-scaler spec: scaleTargetRef: name: llm-server minReplicaCount: 2 maxReplicaCount: 12 cooldownPeriod: 600 triggers: - type: prometheus metadata: serverAddress: http://prometheus.monitoring:9090 query: sum(vllm:num_requests_waiting) threshold: \"8\" Cold start Adding capacity needs a new GPU node, then an image pull, then a model download and weight load. That can take minutes. Reduce it with pre-pulled images, models on a fast local or shared volume, a pool of warm spare replicas and cluster autoscaler or Karpenter settings that keep GPU nodes ready. Cost control Set a maximum replica count and a budget alert. Use a long scale-down delay so replicas are not removed during short dips, because reloading is costly. For predictable peaks, scale up on a schedule before the peak: kubectl get scaledobject,hpa -n ai kubectl get nodes -l node.kubernetes.io/instance-type=gpu -o wide Review the cost per thousand requests monthly. Incident: Ten minutes of timeouts Incident A traffic spike outran GPU node startup. 09:00 queue depth 340 09:01 scale up 2 -> 8 09:09 replicas ready 429 rate 41% until then Scaling worked, but new replicas needed minutes to start. The team kept a warm pool, moved model files to a local cache, added a scheduled scale-up before the known morning peak and rate-limited low-priority traffic during spikes. Common mistakes Scaling on CPU No warm capacity for slow-starting models Aggressive scale-down that causes reloads No maximum replicas or budget alert"},"gpu-serving/model-loading-caching-and-cold-starts":{"html":"\u003cp>A model server cannot serve until its weights are in GPU memory. Getting there involves pulling a large image, fetching files and moving them across the bus, and each step offers a chance to speed up or to fail.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Where start-up time goes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Caching weights and images close to the GPU\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Readiness and startup probes for model servers\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Rolling updates that keep capacity\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"where-time-goes\">Where time goes\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Pull the container image, often many gigabytes with CUDA libraries.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Download or read the model weights.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Load weights to GPU memory and initialize kernels.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Warm up with a few requests.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>Measure each step with timestamps so you know which to fix.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"caching\">Caching\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"volumes:\n  - name: models\n    persistentVolumeClaim:\n      claimName: model-cache      # fast shared or local NVMe volume\ncontainers:\n  - name: server\n    image: registry.example.com/llm-server:1.8\n    args: [&quot;--model&quot;, &quot;/models/llama-8b&quot;, &quot;--load-format&quot;, &quot;safetensors&quot;]\n    volumeMounts:\n      - {name: models, mountPath: /models, readOnly: true}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">volumes:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">models\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">persistentVolumeClaim:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">claimName:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">model-cache\u003c/span>      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># fast shared or local NVMe volume\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">containers:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">server\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">image:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">registry.example.com/llm-server:1.8\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">args:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;--model&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;/models/llama-8b&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;--load-format&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;safetensors&quot;\u003c/span>]\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">volumeMounts:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">models\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">mountPath:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">/models\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">readOnly:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Use \u003cstrong>safetensors\u003c/strong>, which load quickly and avoid unsafe code execution. Populate the volume once with a job, verify file checksums and mount it read-only. Pre-pull images onto GPU nodes, or use an image cache in the cluster.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"probes-and-rollouts\">Probes and rollouts\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"startupProbe:\n  httpGet: {path: /health, port: 8000}\n  failureThreshold: 60\n  periodSeconds: 10\nreadinessProbe:\n  httpGet: {path: /v1/models, port: 8000}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">startupProbe:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">httpGet:\u003c/span> {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">path:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">/health\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">port:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">8000\u003c/span>}\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">failureThreshold:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">60\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">periodSeconds:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">readinessProbe:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">httpGet:\u003c/span> {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">path:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">/v1/models\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">port:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">8000\u003c/span>}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The startup probe allows a long load without being killed, and readiness keeps traffic away until the model answers. Roll out with \u003ccode>maxUnavailable: 0\u003c/code> and a small \u003ccode>maxSurge\u003c/code>, so old replicas keep serving until new ones are ready. Remember that a surge needs spare GPUs.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-killed-while-still-loading\">Incident: Killed while still loading\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Pods restarted repeatedly because probes were too strict for a slow load.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Liveness probe failed: connection refused\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Container server failed liveness probe, will be restarted\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Back-off restarting failed container (CrashLoopBackOff)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The liveness probe started after 30 seconds, but loading took four minutes, so Kubernetes kept killing the pod just before it was ready. A startup probe with a long allowance and a readiness probe on the model endpoint fixed the loop.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Liveness probes that fire during model loading\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Downloading weights from the internet on every start\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Restarting all replicas together\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Writable model volumes shared by many pods\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"where-time-goes","text":"Where time goes"},{"id":"caching","text":"Caching"},{"id":"probes-and-rollouts","text":"Probes and rollouts"},{"id":"incident-killed-while-still-loading","text":"Incident: Killed while still loading"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Hugging Face safetensors","u":"https://huggingface.co/docs/safetensors/index"},{"t":"Kubernetes probes","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/configure-pod-container/configure-liveness-readiness-startup-probes/"}],"text":"A model server cannot serve until its weights are in GPU memory. Getting there involves pulling a large image, fetching files and moving them across the bus, and each step offers a chance to speed up or to fail. What you will learn Where start-up time goes Caching weights and images close to the GPU Readiness and startup probes for model servers Rolling updates that keep capacity Where time goes Pull the container image, often many gigabytes with CUDA libraries. Download or read the model weights. Load weights to GPU memory and initialize kernels. Warm up with a few requests. Measure each step with timestamps so you know which to fix. Caching volumes: - name: models persistentVolumeClaim: claimName: model-cache # fast shared or local NVMe volume containers: - name: server image: registry.example.com/llm-server:1.8 args: [ \"--model\" , \"/models/llama-8b\" , \"--load-format\" , \"safetensors\" ] volumeMounts: - { name: models , mountPath: /models , readOnly: true } Use safetensors , which load quickly and avoid unsafe code execution. Populate the volume once with a job, verify file checksums and mount it read-only. Pre-pull images onto GPU nodes, or use an image cache in the cluster. Probes and rollouts startupProbe: httpGet: { path: /health , port: 8000 } failureThreshold: 60 periodSeconds: 10 readinessProbe: httpGet: { path: /v1/models , port: 8000 } The startup probe allows a long load without being killed, and readiness keeps traffic away until the model answers. Roll out with maxUnavailable: 0 and a small maxSurge , so old replicas keep serving until new ones are ready. Remember that a surge needs spare GPUs. Incident: Killed while still loading Incident Pods restarted repeatedly because probes were too strict for a slow load. Liveness probe failed: connection refused Container server failed liveness probe, will be restarted Back-off restarting failed container (CrashLoopBackOff) The liveness probe started after 30 seconds, but loading took four minutes, so Kubernetes kept killing the pod just before it was ready. A startup probe with a long allowance and a readiness probe on the model endpoint fixed the loop. Common mistakes Liveness probes that fire during model loading Downloading weights from the internet on every start Restarting all replicas together Writable model volumes shared by many pods"},"gpu-serving/gpu-sharing-mig-and-time-slicing":{"html":"\u003cp>Giving each small job a whole GPU wastes money. Sharing is possible in several ways, and they differ mostly in how well jobs are isolated from each other.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>How MIG partitions a GPU\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How time-slicing and MPS differ\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Requesting shared GPUs in Kubernetes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choosing a method by workload\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"mig\">MIG\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Multi-Instance GPU\u003c/strong> on supported data center GPUs splits a card into up to seven instances, each with its own memory, cache and compute. A fault or heavy use in one instance does not affect others.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"nvidia-smi -i 0 -mig 1\nnvidia-smi mig -cgi 1g.10gb,1g.10gb,2g.20gb -C\nnvidia-smi -L\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">nvidia-smi -i 0 -mig 1\nnvidia-smi mig -cgi 1g.10gb,1g.10gb,2g.20gb -C\nnvidia-smi -L\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>In Kubernetes the GPU Operator advertises slices as resources such as \u003ccode>nvidia.com/mig-1g.10gb\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"resources:\n  limits:\n    nvidia.com/mig-1g.10gb: 1\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">resources:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">limits:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">nvidia.com/mig-1g.10gb:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"time-slicing-and-mps\">Time-slicing and MPS\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Time-slicing\u003c/strong> makes several pods share a GPU by taking turns. It works on any GPU but gives no memory isolation, so one pod can exhaust memory and others fail, and latency is unpredictable.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# device plugin config: advertise each GPU as 4 shares\nsharing:\n  timeSlicing:\n    resources:\n      - name: nvidia.com/gpu\n        replicas: 4\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># device plugin config: advertise each GPU as 4 shares\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">sharing:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">timeSlicing:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">resources:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">nvidia.com/gpu\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">replicas:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">4\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>MPS\u003c/strong> lets processes run kernels at the same time and can raise utilization for many small workloads from one user, with limited isolation.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"choosing\">Choosing\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Use \u003cstrong>MIG\u003c/strong> for multi-tenant or latency-sensitive inference. Use \u003cstrong>time-slicing\u003c/strong> for development, notebooks and tests where interference is acceptable. Use \u003cstrong>whole GPUs\u003c/strong> for large models and training. Monitor per-slice memory and utilization, and set limits so that one team cannot take all slices.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-one-notebook-starved-the-rest\">Incident: One notebook starved the rest\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A shared GPU ran out of memory because one pod took almost all of it.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">CUDA error: out of memory\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">nvidia-smi: 79 GiB used by pid 4412\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">3 neighbouring pods failed to start\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Time-slicing gave four pods one card but no memory limits, and one notebook loaded a huge tensor. The team moved shared workloads to MIG slices with fixed memory and kept time-slicing only for short-lived development sessions with quotas.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using time-slicing for production inference\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting that time-slicing has no memory isolation\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Creating MIG layouts that waste slices\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No quotas on shared GPUs\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"mig","text":"MIG"},{"id":"time-slicing-and-mps","text":"Time-slicing and MPS"},{"id":"choosing","text":"Choosing"},{"id":"incident-one-notebook-starved-the-rest","text":"Incident: One notebook starved the rest"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"NVIDIA MIG user guide","u":"https://docs.nvidia.com/datacenter/tesla/mig-user-guide/"},{"t":"NVIDIA GPU Operator","u":"https://docs.nvidia.com/datacenter/cloud-native/gpu-operator/latest/index.html"}],"text":"Giving each small job a whole GPU wastes money. Sharing is possible in several ways, and they differ mostly in how well jobs are isolated from each other. What you will learn How MIG partitions a GPU How time-slicing and MPS differ Requesting shared GPUs in Kubernetes Choosing a method by workload MIG Multi-Instance GPU on supported data center GPUs splits a card into up to seven instances, each with its own memory, cache and compute. A fault or heavy use in one instance does not affect others. nvidia-smi -i 0 -mig 1 nvidia-smi mig -cgi 1g.10gb,1g.10gb,2g.20gb -C nvidia-smi -L In Kubernetes the GPU Operator advertises slices as resources such as nvidia.com/mig-1g.10gb . resources: limits: nvidia.com/mig-1g.10gb: 1 Time-slicing and MPS Time-slicing makes several pods share a GPU by taking turns. It works on any GPU but gives no memory isolation, so one pod can exhaust memory and others fail, and latency is unpredictable. # device plugin config: advertise each GPU as 4 shares sharing: timeSlicing: resources: - name: nvidia.com/gpu replicas: 4 MPS lets processes run kernels at the same time and can raise utilization for many small workloads from one user, with limited isolation. Choosing Use MIG for multi-tenant or latency-sensitive inference. Use time-slicing for development, notebooks and tests where interference is acceptable. Use whole GPUs for large models and training. Monitor per-slice memory and utilization, and set limits so that one team cannot take all slices. Incident: One notebook starved the rest Incident A shared GPU ran out of memory because one pod took almost all of it. CUDA error: out of memory nvidia-smi: 79 GiB used by pid 4412 3 neighbouring pods failed to start Time-slicing gave four pods one card but no memory limits, and one notebook loaded a huge tensor. The team moved shared workloads to MIG slices with fixed memory and kept time-slicing only for short-lived development sessions with quotas. Common mistakes Using time-slicing for production inference Forgetting that time-slicing has no memory isolation Creating MIG layouts that waste slices No quotas on shared GPUs"},"gpu-serving/gpu-failures-xid-errors-and-recovery":{"html":"\u003cp>GPU hardware is dense, hot and heavily used, so failures are normal. The kernel and NVIDIA tools report what went wrong, if you know where to look and what to do next.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Reading Xid messages and ECC counters\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Thermal, power and link problems\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Draining a node and resetting or replacing a GPU\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Automating detection and isolation\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"where-to-look\">Where to look\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"dmesg -T | grep -i &quot;NVRM: Xid&quot;\nnvidia-smi -q -d ECC | grep -E &quot;Uncorrectable|Correctable&quot; | head\nnvidia-smi --query-gpu=index,temperature.gpu,power.draw,clocks_throttle_reasons.active --format=csv\ndcgmi diag -r 2\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">dmesg -T | grep -i \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;NVRM: Xid&quot;\u003c/span>\nnvidia-smi -q -d ECC | grep -E \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Uncorrectable|Correctable&quot;\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span>\nnvidia-smi --query-gpu=index,temperature.gpu,power.draw,clocks_throttle_reasons.active --format=csv\ndcgmi diag -r 2\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>An \u003cstrong>Xid\u003c/strong> is an error code from the driver. Some point to an application bug, such as an illegal memory access, but others point to hardware: uncorrectable memory errors, a GPU that fell off the bus or repeated link errors. Look up the code in NVIDIA&#39;s table before acting.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"fault-families\">Fault families\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Memory:\u003c/strong> rising uncorrectable ECC errors or row remapping failures mean the GPU should be retired.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Thermal and power:\u003c/strong> throttling reasons, high temperature and power capping point to cooling or power limits in the rack.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Link:\u003c/strong> NVLink or PCIe errors can come from a seating problem, a cable or a failing board.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Driver and software:\u003c/strong> hangs after upgrades or container mismatches are fixed by matching driver and CUDA versions.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"respond\">Respond\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl cordon gpu-node-7\nkubectl drain gpu-node-7 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data\nnvidia-smi --gpu-reset -i 3      # only if no process uses the GPU\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl cordon gpu-node-7\nkubectl drain gpu-node-7 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data\nnvidia-smi --gpu-reset -i 3      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># only if no process uses the GPU\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Cordon and drain before touching hardware. A reset can clear transient faults, but repeated Xid errors or ECC failures mean replacement. Record the serial number and evidence for the vendor.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"automate\">Automate\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Run DCGM health checks as a daemon set, export metrics to Prometheus and alert on Xid counts. A controller can taint a failing node automatically so new jobs avoid it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-gpu-that-fell-off-the-bus\">Incident: A GPU that fell off the bus\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A node lost one GPU in the middle of a job.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NVRM: Xid (PCI:0000:3b:00): 79, GPU has fallen off the bus.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">nvidia-smi: Unable to determine the device handle for GPU 3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">job failed at step 18402\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Xid 79 meant the card disappeared from the PCIe bus. A reboot brought it back briefly, but the error returned under load. The team drained the node, reseated and then replaced the GPU, and added an automatic taint on Xid 79 and 48 so jobs no longer landed there.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Treating every Xid as a software bug\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Resetting repeatedly instead of replacing\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Touching hardware before draining the node\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No alert on Xid or ECC counters\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"where-to-look","text":"Where to look"},{"id":"fault-families","text":"Fault families"},{"id":"respond","text":"Respond"},{"id":"automate","text":"Automate"},{"id":"incident-a-gpu-that-fell-off-the-bus","text":"Incident: A GPU that fell off the bus"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"NVIDIA Xid errors","u":"https://docs.nvidia.com/deploy/xid-errors/index.html"},{"t":"NVIDIA DCGM","u":"https://docs.nvidia.com/datacenter/dcgm/latest/index.html"}],"text":"GPU hardware is dense, hot and heavily used, so failures are normal. The kernel and NVIDIA tools report what went wrong, if you know where to look and what to do next. What you will learn Reading Xid messages and ECC counters Thermal, power and link problems Draining a node and resetting or replacing a GPU Automating detection and isolation Where to look dmesg -T | grep -i \"NVRM: Xid\" nvidia-smi -q -d ECC | grep -E \"Uncorrectable|Correctable\" | head nvidia-smi --query-gpu=index,temperature.gpu,power.draw,clocks_throttle_reasons.active --format=csv dcgmi diag -r 2 An Xid is an error code from the driver. Some point to an application bug, such as an illegal memory access, but others point to hardware: uncorrectable memory errors, a GPU that fell off the bus or repeated link errors. Look up the code in NVIDIA&#39;s table before acting. Fault families Memory: rising uncorrectable ECC errors or row remapping failures mean the GPU should be retired. Thermal and power: throttling reasons, high temperature and power capping point to cooling or power limits in the rack. Link: NVLink or PCIe errors can come from a seating problem, a cable or a failing board. Driver and software: hangs after upgrades or container mismatches are fixed by matching driver and CUDA versions. Respond kubectl cordon gpu-node-7 kubectl drain gpu-node-7 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data nvidia-smi --gpu-reset -i 3 # only if no process uses the GPU Cordon and drain before touching hardware. A reset can clear transient faults, but repeated Xid errors or ECC failures mean replacement. Record the serial number and evidence for the vendor. Automate Run DCGM health checks as a daemon set, export metrics to Prometheus and alert on Xid counts. A controller can taint a failing node automatically so new jobs avoid it. Incident: A GPU that fell off the bus Incident A node lost one GPU in the middle of a job. NVRM: Xid (PCI:0000:3b:00): 79, GPU has fallen off the bus. nvidia-smi: Unable to determine the device handle for GPU 3 job failed at step 18402 Xid 79 meant the card disappeared from the PCIe bus. A reboot brought it back briefly, but the error returned under load. The team drained the node, reseated and then replaced the GPU, and added an automatic taint on Xid 79 and 48 so jobs no longer landed there. Common mistakes Treating every Xid as a software bug Resetting repeatedly instead of replacing Touching hardware before draining the node No alert on Xid or ECC counters"}});